  "G1526": {
    "orig": "εἰσί",
    "tran": "eisi",
    "phon": "i-see'",
    "thad": "1. son, ser, fue, etc.\n\n2. These verbs are called linking verbs because they link or connect the subject of a sentence to additional information.\n\n3. They often appear in sentences that describe physical or mental states, conditions, or emotions.\n\n4. Some examples of linking verbs include:\n\n5. are\n6. am\n7. is\n8. was\n9. were\n10. be\n\n11. These verbs can also be used in other ways, such as to indicate a change in state or to show that something is happening now.\n\n12. For example:\n\n13. The sun is shining.\n14. She will be happy tomorrow.\n15. They are tired from their trip.\n\n16. Linking verbs can also be used to make questions or negatives.\n\n17. For instance:\n\n18. Is she going to the store?\n19. He is not going to the party.\n20. We are not sure about the answer.",
    "orin": "third person plural present indicative of G1510",
    "tdnt": "None",
    "posp": "Verbo",
    "sdef": "Tercera persona plural indicativo presente de G1510;\nellos son: - acordar, son, ser, durar, no hay X es, fueron.",
    "atra": "eisi3",
    "shor": "Son, ser, fue, etc."
  },
