  "G1683": {
    "orig": "ἐμαυτοῦ ἐμαυτῷ ἐμαυτόν",
    "tran": "emautou emautō emauton",
    "phon": "em-ow-too'",
    "thad": "1. Yo, mí, mismo etc.\n\n2. The use of personal pronouns is one of the most basic and fundamental aspects of language.\n\n3. However, their meaning can be quite complex and context-dependent.\n\n4. This is because they often refer to different entities or concepts depending on the situation.\n\n5. For example, \"I\" typically refers to the speaker themselves, while \"me\" usually refers to the object of a preposition or verb.\n\n6. But in certain contexts, such as in questions like \"What do you think I/ me would do?\", both \"I\" and \"me\" can refer to the same entity - the speaker.\n\n7. Similarly, \"myself\" often emphasizes the speaker's involvement or responsibility.\n\n8. In other cases, however, \"myself\" may refer to a part of the speaker's body or their own personal interests.\n\n9. The ambiguity surrounding these pronouns can sometimes lead to misunderstandings or misinterpretations.\n\n10. Therefore, it is essential to consider the context and potential ambiguities when using personal pronouns in language.",
    "orin": "genitive case compound of G1700 and G846",
    "tdnt": "None",
    "posp": "",
    "sdef": "Genitivo, dativo y acusativo de un compuesto de G1700 y G846; de mí mismo: - yo, propio mío (mismo), yo mismo.",
    "atra": "emautou hs ou",
    "shor": "Yo, mí, mismo etc."
  },
